!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/40/io/ltr8/http/service.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/meta.tn"
@doc:"""
  A vocabulary for describing what a service can be asked to do: exchanges, grouped into protocols, and the
  facts each exchange states about itself. A schema importing this one is a service description. Nothing
  here names a transport: how HTTP exposes a protocol is `http.tn`, which imports this schema, and a
  description using HTTP imports that one alone and sees this one's names through its import closure.

  ONE ROOT NAME. Every entry of this layer, its annotations included, is a member of the schema map
  `service` nested in its root, and is named by its path from the root: `service.protocol`,
  `service.exchange`, `@service.summary`. Every root name of an import closure is reserved in each importer
  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3), and a description imports this layer beside the domain schemas it describes,
  so a layer declaring `protocol` or `exchange` at its root would forbid a domain those names. Nested in
  one map, it reserves `service` only against a root that is no schema map: a nested schema map is open,
  so another schema's `service => !schema { ... }` adds its members to this one, and only a path both
  declare collides. An entry refers to another by the same path, from inside the map as from outside it,
  since a name resolves in the schema's one namespace.

  The layer describes a service from the protocol's side, not the host language's. A protocol is a named
  set of exchanges, each one request-and-reply: a request record, a response record and error records. A
  language is reached from that side, through the join (`design/invoke.md`), and no parameter list, return
  type or exception is ever written here.

  `service.protocol` is a RECORD holding one keyed field, `exchanges`, a namespace ([TSON-SCHEMA] §4.1) --
  a `service.exchanges` mapping exchange names to exchanges -- since a reference walks into it. An entry
  applying it is a value, never a type. `service.exchange` and `service.signature` inside it are ordinary
  records too.

    orders => !service.protocol {
      exchanges: {
        @service.safe get_order => { request: order_ref  response: order  errors: [order_not_found] }
        place_order             => { request: new_order  response: order  errors: [sku_not_found] }
      }
    }

  Exchanges are keys inside their protocol's namespace, so none is a global entry: two protocols may both
  declare `get_order`. An exchange's qualified name is its protocol's entry, the field `exchanges`, then
  its key: `orders.exchanges.get_order`. A protocol lists its exchanges and nothing more; there is no
  inheritance between protocols.

  THE POSITIONAL FORM, AND WHAT IT COSTS. `service.protocol` has exactly one bare REQUIRED field, so
  [TSON-SCHEMA] §5.6 lets the map stand in for it: `orders => !service.protocol { get_order => ... }` is
  `orders => !service.protocol { exchanges: { get_order => ... } }`. The examples write a protocol in full,
  since a reference into it spells `exchanges` whether the document wrote it or not. The cost is a
  versioning one and it is the reason this is written down: adding a SECOND required field withdraws the
  positional form, and every document written in it stops parsing. So a new field there is optional or it
  is a new type.

  WHAT THE RESOLVER CHECKS, AND WHAT THE READER DOES. A relation that names something is a reference the
  resolver judges at schema load: each type a signature names is a `meta.type_ref`, and a reference to an
  exchange from elsewhere is a `meta.ref<service.exchange>` walking an entry, its `exchanges` field and a
  key. What relates two things beyond naming one is the reader's: an exchange marked both
  `@service.safe` and `@service.destructive` is refused when `TsonServiceDescription` reads an http
  projection binding its protocol.
  """
!schema {
  service => !schema {
    @doc:"""
      The transport-neutral contract of one request-and-reply. A plain record on purpose: it is what
      `exchange` and an inline http `operation` share.

      `request` absent means the exchange takes nothing; `response` absent means it returns nothing.
      `errors` lists BUSINESS errors -- the request was schema-valid and the domain said no -- each a type
      composing `problem.details`. A transport's own refusals (a body failing its schema, an Accept it
      cannot satisfy) are not listed: every exchange has them and the error boundary answers them.
      """
    signature => {
      request?:         meta.type_ref
      response?:        meta.type_ref
      errors?:          [meta.type_ref]
    }

    @doc:"""
      An exchange: one request-and-reply, a signature and nothing more. Its NAME is the key it sits under in a
      protocol. A record, not a constructor: it is only ever a map value. The empty trailing body is what
      makes it a type of its own -- `exchange => service.signature` would be an alias, and an alias is a hop to its
      target, so the values would read back as signatures.
      """
    exchange => service.signature & {}

    @doc:"""
      A protocol's exchanges, keyed by name. A namespace type, so a qualified name enters it: a
      `meta.ref<service.exchange>` reaches an exchange as `orders.exchanges.place_order`, the protocol's entry,
      this field and then the key. An exchange is a map VALUE under its name, so its name is scoped to the
      protocol, and annotations written before the key -- `@doc`, `@service.summary`, `@service.safe`,
      `@service.idempotent`, `@service.destructive` -- document it and state its facts.
      """
    exchanges => !meta.namespace { key_type: service.exchange_name  value_type: service.exchange }

    @doc:"""
      A protocol: a named set of exchanges, and nothing else. A record whose one field is the namespace of its
      exchanges, so a fact about the protocol as a whole is a field beside it, where a value of a namespace type
      is the map alone. Its one required field is bare, so the positional form admits the map written alone,
      and a second required field would withdraw it.
      """
    protocol => {
      exchanges: service.exchanges
    }

    @doc:"""
      The naming role of the namespace this layer declares, as the kernel declares `meta.type_name` and
      `meta.param_name` over `meta.identifier`. A key typed by a role enforces that namespace's grammar and
      names the namespace in the refusal ("'exchange_name': 'place order': U+0020 cannot appear in an
      identifier"), where `type_name` would misname the namespace doing the refusing. A namespace type's key is
      a name, so its role is an identifier family: where an authority's grammar is wider than TSON's
      identifier, the role is a `unicode.identifier_type` widened with `start_add` and `continue_add`.

      An exchange's name: an identifier in its protocol's namespace, not a type name.
      """
    exchange_name => meta.identifier

    @doc:"""
      The short form of an exchange's or an endpoint's documentation, written before its key --
      `@service.summary:"Place an order." place_order => { ... }`. Declared as the kernel declares `doc`;
      `@doc` remains the long form. Not a field, because documentation is metadata about a declaration rather
      than part of what it declares, and an annotation type declared here reaches every schema importing
      this one, `http.tn` among them.
      """
    summary => @meta.annotation text

    @doc:"""
      The exchange has no effect a caller can observe: calling it is a read. `safe` is the first of three facts
      about an exchange that a caller composing calls needs -- an agent planning a batch, deciding what may be
      retried, reordered or must be confirmed first -- and has no HTTP verb to read them from; `idempotent` and
      `destructive`, declared after it, are the other two. Bare markers before the exchange's key:
      `@service.safe get_order => { ... }`, `@service.idempotent cancel_order => { ... }`. `@service.safe` implies
      `@service.idempotent`. Annotations rather than fields because they describe the exchange, not its request or
      response; an http projection CHECKS its verb against them (GET must be safe; PUT and DELETE idempotent) and
      never derives one from the other. `safe` and `idempotent` are RFC 9110's words for the same facts.

      `@service.destructive` is the third: the exchange has an effect a caller cannot undo, and a caller should
      confirm before invoking it -- `@service.idempotent @service.destructive cancel_order => { ... }`. It is the one
      fact an LLM tool-calling convention asks for that the other two do not state. `@service.safe` and
      `@service.destructive` exclude each other, since a safe exchange has no effect to undo, and an exchange marked
      both is refused when an http projection binding its protocol is read. Nothing derives it from a verb and no verb
      implies it: a `DELETE` may cancel a reservation that can be made again, and a `POST` may wipe a table. An http
      projection refuses a destructive exchange bound at GET or HEAD, as it refuses an unsafe one there.
      """
    safe        => @meta.annotation void

    @doc:"The exchange may be repeated with the effect of one call, so a caller may retry it; `@service.safe` implies it."
    idempotent  => @meta.annotation void

    @doc:"The exchange has an effect a caller cannot undo, so a caller confirms it first; it excludes `@service.safe`."
    destructive => @meta.annotation void
  }
}
